e four remaining points are \\((1,1),(1,3),(2,1),(3,1)\\). The only sunny pair among them is \\((1,3)\\) with \\((2,1)\\) (slope \\(-2\\)). After using that line, the points \\((1,1)\\) and \\((3,1)\\) are left, but any line through them is horizontal (non\u2011sunny). Hence impossible.  \n   - \\(y=3\\) covers only \\((1,3)\\). The five remaining points cannot be covered by two sunny lines because \\((2,1)\\) cannot be paired with any other remaining point without obtaining a forbidden slope.  \n\n   *Vertical line.*  \n   - \\(x=1\\) covers \\((1,1),(1,2),(1,3)\\). The remaining points \\((2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\) are pairwise either vertical, horizontal or slope \\(-1\\); thus no sunny line can contain two of them. Two sunny lines can cover at most two of the three points \u2013 impossible.  \n   - \\(x=2\\) covers \\((2,1),(2,2)\\). The other four points are \\((1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(3,1)\\). The only sunny pair among them is \\((1,3)\\) with \\((3,1)\\) (slope \\(-1\\), which is **not** sunny). Hence every pair gives a forbidden slope, so the four points need at least three sunny lines \u2013 impossible.  \n\n   *Slope \\(-1\\) line.*  \n   - The only lines of slope \\(-1\\) that pass through points of \\(T_3\\) are \\(x+y=2\\) (through \\((1,1)\\)), \\(x+y=3\\) (through \\((1,2),(2,1)\\)) and \\(x+y=4\\) (through \\((1,3),(2,2),(3,1)\\)).  \n   - If we take \\(x+y=3\\) (covers two points) the remaining four points are \\((1,1),(1,3),(2,2),(3,1)\\). The only sunny pair among them is \\((1,1)\\) with \\((2,2)\\) (slope \\(1\\)); the other two points \\((1,3)\\) and \\((3,1)\\) cannot be joined by a sunny line (their slope is \\(-1\\)). Hence we would need three sunny lines \u2013 contradiction.  \n   - If we take \\(x+y=4\\) (covers three points) the uncovered points are \\((1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\). Any line through two of these has slope \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\); thus at most one of them can be covered by a sunny line, leaving at least one point uncovered.  \n\n   In every possible placement of the non\u2011sunny line we arrive at a contradiction.\n\n4. **Conclusion.**  \n   Because a sunny line can contain at most two points of \\(T_3\\) and every possible non\u2011sunny line leaves a set of remaining points that cannot be covered by only two sunny lines, a configuration with exactly two sunny lines does **not** exist.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For }n=3\\text{, }k=2\\text{ is impossible.}}\n\\]"
