ssibility of \\(k=2\\) when \\(n=3\\).**  \n   The set \\(T_{3}\\) consists of the six points  \n   \\[\n   (1,1),\\;(1,2),\\;(2,1),\\;(1,3),\\;(2,2),\\;(3,1).\n   \\]  \n   Suppose we had three distinct lines, exactly two of which are sunny, covering all six points.  \n\n   *Each sunny line can meet each of the three diagonals \\(x+y=2,3,4\\) in at most one point, because a line not parallel to a diagonal intersects it in a single point.  Consequently a sunny line contains at most three points of \\(T_{3}\\).*\n\n   *If both sunny lines each contain three points, then their point‑sets must be disjoint (otherwise the total number of distinct covered points would be at most five).  In that case the remaining non‑sunny line would be forced to pass through a point already covered, contradicting the requirement that the three lines be distinct.*\n\n   *If at least one sunny line contains at most two points, then the total number of points covered by the three lines is at most \\(2+3+3=8\\), but because the two sunny lines share at most one point (otherwise the non‑sunny line would be forced to be parallel to a diagonal and could cover at most three points), the number of *distinct* points covered is at most five.*\n\n   In every possible arrangement we obtain fewer than six distinct points, contradicting the hypothesis.  Hence no configuration with exactly two sunny lines exists for \\(n=3\\).\n\n6. **Partial conclusion.**  \n   From the constructions in §§2–4 we have shown that for every \\(n\\ge 3\\) the values \\(k=0,1,n\\) are realizable.  \n   From §5 we have shown that for \\(n=3\\) the value \\(k=2\\) is impossible, so the admissible set for \\(n=3\\) is \\(\\{0,1,3\\}\\).  \n\n   The existence (or non‑existence) of configurations with intermediate numbers of sunny lines \\(2\\le k\\le n-1\\) when \\(n\\ge 4\\) remains unresolved by the present argument.\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary**