            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n###\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  The case \\(k=0\\)\n\nThe \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nare pairwise distinct, none of them is sunny, and each point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) satisfies \\(a\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\); hence it lies on the line \\(x=a\\).  Thus \\(k=0\\) is always realizable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  A triangular block covered by sunny lines  \n\nFor an integer \\(m\\ge 1\\) define  \n\n\\[\nT_m=\\{(u,v)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid u+v\\le m+1\\}.\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Lemma 1.**  \nFor every \\(m\\ge 1\\) there exist exactly \\(m\\) distinct sunny lines whose union contains all points of \\(T_m\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nInduction on \\(m\\).\n\n*Base \\(m=1\\).*  \\(T_1=\\{(1,1)\\}\\).  The line \\(y=x\\) (slope \\(1\\)) is sunny and contains the point.\n\n*Inductive step.*  Assume the statement true for \\(m-1\\).  \nConsider the line  \n\n\\[\nL_m:\\; y = x-(m-1).\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nIts slope is \\(1\\); therefore \\(L_m\\) is sunny.  \nThe points of \\(T_m\\) lying on \\(L_m\\) are precisely  \n\n\\[\n(1,m),\\;(2,m-1),\\dots ,\\;(m,1),\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\ni.e. the “main diagonal’’ of the triangle; there are exactly \\(m\\) of them.\n\nRemoving the points (9) from \\(T_m\\) leaves  \n\n\\[\nT_{m-1}=\\{(u,v)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid u+v\\le m\\},\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nwhich is a triangle of size \\(m-1\\).  By the induction hypothesis \\(T_{m-1}\\) can be covered by \\(m-1\\) sunny lines.  Together with \\(L_m\\) we obtain \\(m\\) sunny lines covering all of \\(T_m\\). ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Construction for arbitrary \\((n,k)\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\)\n\nFix \\(n\\ge 3\\) and \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).\n\n*Step 1.*  Use the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-k .\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nThese are non‑sunny and cover every point of \\(S_n\\) whose \\(x\\)-coordinate does not exceed \\(n-k\\).\n\n*Step 2.*  The uncovered points form the set  \n\n\\[\nS_n'=\\{(a,b)\\in S_n\\mid a\\ge n-k+1\\}.\n\\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nWrite \\(a'=a-(n-k)\\) and keep \\(b'=b\\).  Then \\(a',b'\\ge 1\\) and  \n\n\\[\na'+b'\\le k+1,\n\\]\n\nso \\((a',b')\\in T_k\\).  Hence \\(S_n'\\) is a translate of the triangle \\(T_k\\).\n\nApply Lemma 1 to \\(T_k\\) and translate the obtained \\(k\\) sunny lines back by the vector \\((n-k,0)\\).  The resulting \\(k\\) lines are sunny (translation does not change slope) and together cover all points of \\(S_n'\\).\n\nThus we have produced exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, precisely \\(k\\) of them sunny, covering the whole set \\(S_n\\).  Consequently\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For every }n\\ge 3\\text{ and every }k\\text{ with }0\\le k\\le n\\text{ a configuration exists,}\n}\n\\tag{13}\n\\]\n\n*except* possibly for the smallest value of \\(n\\) where the inductive construction of Lemma 1 fails to start, namely \\(n=3\\) with \\(k=2\\).  The next section treats this exceptional case.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  The exceptional case \\(n=3\\)\n\nThe set \\(S_3\\) consists of the six points listed in (3).\n\n*Existence for \\(k=0,1,3\\).*  \nThe explicit families of lines given in the summary are verified point‑by‑point; each family contains exactly the required number of sunny lines.\n\n*Impossibility of \\(k=2\\).*  \n\n1. **Maximum size of a sunny line.**  \n   The only lines that contain three points of \\(S_3\\) are the three non‑sunny lines (4).  \n   Hence any sunny line contains at most two points of \\(S_3\\).\n\n2. **Counting argument.**  \n   With two sunny lines we can cover at most \\(2+2=4\\) points.  \n   The remaining two points must be covered by the single non‑sunny line.  \n   Every non‑sunny line that contains two points of \\(S_3\\) actually contains a third point (again by (4)).  \n   Consequently the non‑sunny line would contain at least three points, forcing at least one of those three to be already covered by a sunny line.  That would leave at most one uncovered point, contradicting the fact that after the two sunny lines we still have two uncovered points.\n\n3. **Exhaustive verification.**  \n   One can list the \\(\\binom{6}{2}=15\\) unordered pairs of points.  For each pair the unique line through it is either  \n   – vertical, horizontal or of slope \\(-1\\) (hence non‑sunny), or  \n   – has slope \\(0,\\,\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\) and therefore contains a third point of \\(S_3\\).  \n   No pair yields a sunny line that leaves a complementary pair lying on a single non‑sunny line.  \n   Hence no arrangement with exactly two sunny lines can cover all six points.\n\nTherefore a configuration with \\(k=2\\) does **not** exist for \\(n=3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Final answer\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{aligned}\n&\\text{If }n=3,\\quad k\\in\\{0,1,3\\};\\\\[2mm]\n&\\text{If }n\\g