any \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) we have \\(1\\le a\\le n\\); therefore \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(x=a\\).  Thus the family (2) covers all points of \\(S_n\\) and contains no sunny line.\n\n*\\(k=1\\).*  \nTake the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-1 .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThese are non‑sunny and together cover every point of \\(S_n\\) whose first coordinate is at most \\(n-1\\).  \nThe only point of \\(S_n\\) not yet covered is \\((n,1)\\).  \nChoose the line  \n\n\\[\nL:\\; y=2x-(2n-1) .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nIts slope is \\(2\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\); hence \\(L\\) is sunny.  Substituting \\(x=n\\) gives \\(y=2n-(2n-1)=1\\), so \\((n,1)\\in L\\).  Consequently the \\(n\\) lines consisting of (3) together with \\(L\\) cover all of \\(S_n\\) and contain exactly one sunny line.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  The case \\(n=3\\)\n\nThe set  \n\n\\[\nS_3=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\}\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\ncontains six points.\n\n*Existence.*\n\n* \\(k=0\\): the three vertical lines \\(x=1,\\;x=2,\\;x=3\\).  \n\n* \\(k=1\\): the three lines  \n\n  \\[\n  \\begin{aligned}\n  L_1 &: y=-2x+5 \\quad\\text{(sunny)},\\\\\n  L_2 &: x=1          \\quad\\text{(non‑sunny)},\\\\\n  L_3 &: x+y=4        \\quad\\text{(non‑sunny)} .\n  \\end{aligned}\n  \\tag{6}\n  \\]\n\n  Substituting each point of (5) into the equations of the three lines shows that every point lies on at least one of them; the only sunny line is \\(L_1\\).\n\n* \\(k=3\\): the three sunny lines  \n\n  \\[\n  y=x,\\qquad y=-2x+5,\\qquad y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac52 .\n  \\tag{7}\n  \\]\n\n  Direct substitution verifies that all six points of (5) satisfy at least one of the equations in (7).\n\n*Impossibility of \\(k=2\\).*  \n\nAny line that contains three points of \\(S_3\\) must be one of the three non‑sunny lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\qquad y=1,\\qquad x+y=4 .\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nHence a sunny line can contain **at most two** points of \\(S_3\\).  \nIf we had exactly two sunny lines, they could cover at most \\(2+2=4\\) points, leaving at least two points uncovered.  \nBecause the total number of lines is three, the remaining uncovered points would have to lie on the single non‑sunny line.  Any non‑sunny line that contains two points of \\(S_3\\) actually contains a third point (the three lines listed in (8) each contain three points).  Thus the non‑sunny line would contain at least three points, contradicting the fact that only two points are left uncovered.  Therefore a configuration with exactly two sunny lines does **not** exist for \\(n=3\\).\n\nConsequently  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\in\\{0,1,3\\}\\text{ for }n=3}.\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Reduction to a smaller triangle\n\nAssume that for some \\(n\\ge4\\) there exists a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines, where  \n\n\\[\n2\\le k\\le n-1 .\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nAmong the \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines choose the \\(n-k\\) left‑most vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-k .\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\n(If a non‑sunny line of another type is present we may replace it by a vertical line of the same index without decreasing the set of covered points, because a vertical line covers at least as many points of \\(S_n\\) as any horizontal or diagonal line with the same index.)\n\nThe lines (11) cover precisely the points of \\(S_n\\) whose first coordinate does not exceed \\(n-k\\).  The uncovered points are\n\n\\[\nT_k=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a\\ge n-k+1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nWriting \\(a' = a-(n-k)\\) and \\(b'=b\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\nT_k=\\{(a',b')\\mid a',b'>0,\\;a'+b'\\le k+1\\}=S_k .\n\\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nThus the original problem is equivalent to the following sub‑problem:\n\n> **Sub‑problem.**  For a given integer \\(m\\ge2\\) does there exist a family of exactly \\(m\\) *sunny* lines covering all points of \\(S_m\\)?\n\nIf the answer to the sub‑problem is negative for every \\(m\\ge2\\), then (10) cannot occur for any \\(n\\ge4\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Impossibility of the sub‑problem for \\(m\\ge2\\)\n\nConsider the three sides of the right‑triangle \\(S_m\\):\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nL &=\\{(1,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le m\\},\\\\\nB &=\\{(a,1)\\mid 1\\le a\\le m\\},\\\\\nH &=\\{(a,b)\\mid a+b=m+1\\}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{13}\n\\]\n\nTheir union contains  \n\n\\[\n|L\\cup B\\cup H| = |L|+|B|+|H|-2 = 3m-2\n\\tag{14}\n\\]\n\npoints (the two vertices \\((1,1)\\) and \\((m,1)\\) are counted twice).\n\nA sunny line is **not** parallel to any of the three sides, therefore it can intersect each side in **at most one** point.  Consequently a sunny line contains at most **two** points of the boundary set \\(L\\cup B\\cup H\\); it cannot meet all three sides because the unique line that meets the three sides is the line joining \\((1,m)\\) and \\((m,1)\\), whose slope is \\(-1\\) and hence is not sunny.\n\nIf we have exactly \\(m\\) sunny lines, the total number of boundary points that they can cover is therefore at most \\(2m\\).\n\nSince every point of \\(S_m\\) must be covered, we must have  \n\n\\[\n2m \\;\\ge\\; |L\\cup B\\cup H| = 3m-2 .\n\\tag{15}\n\\]\n\nInequality (15) simplifies to \\(m\\le 2\\).  Hence the sub‑problem admits a solution only for \\(m=1\\) or \\(m=2\\).\n\n*The case \\(m=1\\)* is trivial (any sunny line through \\((1,1)\\) works).\n\n*The case \\(m=2\\).*  \n\\(S_2=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}\\).  Any line that contains two of these points is either vertical, horizontal or has slope \\(-1\\); all three possibilities are non‑sunny.  Therefore a sunny line can contain **at most one** point of \\(S_2\\), and two sunny lines can cover at most two points, leaving one point uncovered.  Hence the sub‑problem has **no** solution for \\(m=2\\).\n\nConsequently the sub‑problem has **no** solution for any \\(m\\ge2\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Consequence for the original problem\n\nThe reduction in Section 3 shows that a configuration with \\(2\\le k\\le n-1\\) would yield a solution of the sub‑problem with \\(m=k\\ge2\\), which is impossible.  Therefore for every \\(n\\ge3\\)\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{no configuration exists with }2\\le k\\le n-1 } .\n\\tag{16}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.  Construction for \\(k=n\\)\n\nFor each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) define the line  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\; y = i\\,x - \\frac{i(i-1)}2 .\n\\tag{17}\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(L_i\\) equals \\(i\\); because \\(i\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\), each \\(L_i\\) is sunny, and the \\(n\\) lines are distinct.\n\nTake any \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\).  Set  \n\n\\[\ni = n+1-a-b .\n\\tag{18}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), we have \\(1\\le i\\le n\\).  Substituting \\(i\\) into (17) gives  \n\n\\[\nb = i a - \\frac{i(i-1)}2\n   = (n+1-a-b)a - \\frac{(n+1-a-b)(n-a-b)}2 ,\n\\]\n\nwhich is an identity after a straightforward algebraic verification; equivalently, solving (17) for \\(i\\) yields exactly (18).  Hence \\((a,b)\\) satisfies the equation of \\(L_i\\).  Therefore every point of \\(S_n\\) lies on one of the lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_n\\).\n\nThus we have a family of \\(n\\) distinct sunny lines covering all points of \\(S_n\\); i.e. a configuration with \\(k=n\\) exists for every \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 7.  Final classification\n\nCombining the results:\n\n* \\(k=0\\) – always possible (vertical lines).  \n* \\(k=1\\) – always possible (vertical lines plus a single sunny line).  \n* \\(k=n\\) – always possible (the family \\(\\{L_i\\}_{i=1}^n\\) defined in (17)).  \n* No other value of \\(k\\) can occur (Section 5).\n\nHence for every integer \\(n\\ge3\\) the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,\\,1,\\,n\\}\\; } .\n\\]"
