nsider the \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\). \u2026 every \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) satisfies \\(1\\le a\\le n\\), hence lies on \\(x=a\\).\u201d\n\n*Correct.*  All points are covered, and each line is non\u2011sunny (parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis).  \n\n---\n\n### 4. Construction for \\(k=1\\)\n\n> \u201cTake the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\) and the line \\(L: y=2x-(2n-1)\\). \u2026 \\((n,1)\\in L\\).\u201d\n\n*Correct.*  The vertical lines cover all points with first coordinate \\(\\le n-1\\); the only uncovered point is \\((n,1)\\), which lies on the sunny line \\(L\\) (slope \\(2\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\)).  \n\n---\n\n### 5. The case \\(n=3\\)\n\n> \u201c\\(S_3=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\}\\). \u2026 Constructions for \\(k=0,1,3\\) are given and verified point\u2011by\u2011point. \u2026 Impossibility of \\(k=2\\) is argued by noting that any line containing three points of \\(S_3\\) must be one of the three non\u2011sunny lines, so a sunny line can contain at most two points; two sunny lines would leave at least two points uncovered, which cannot be covered by a single non\u2011sunny line without also covering a third point, a contradiction.\u201d\n\n*Correct.*  The enumeration of \\(S_3\\) is accurate, the three explicit families of lines indeed cover all six points, and the impossibility argument for \\(k=2\\) is rigorous.\n\n---\n\n### 6. Reduction to a smaller triangle  \n\n> \u201cAssume a configuration with \\(2\\le k\\le n-1\\). Among the \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines choose the \\(n-k\\) left\u2011most vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-k\\). (If a non\u2011sunny line of another type is present we may replace it by a vertical line of the same index without decreasing the set of covered points, because a vertical line covers at least as many points of \\(S_n\\) as any horizontal or diagonal line with the same index.)\u201d\n\n**Issue \u2013 Justification Gap.**  \nThe claim that any non\u2011sunny line (horizontal \\(y=c\\