n**Issue \u2013 Critical Error.**  \nSince the impossibility claim for the sub\u2011problem is false, the derived impossibility for the original problem is also false. Explicit counter\u2011examples exist:\n\n* For \\(n=4\\) the following four distinct lines work:\n  * non\u2011sunny: \\(x=1\\);\n  * sunny: \\(y=x-1,\\; y=-2x+7,\\; y=-\\tfrac12x+3\\).\n  These three sunny lines are precisely the \\(S_3\\) construction shifted by one unit, covering the uncovered points; together with the vertical line they cover all of \\(S_4\\). Hence \\(k=3\\) (which lies between \\(2\\) and \\(n-1\\)) is attainable.\n\n* For \\(n=5\\) the five lines\n  * non\u2011sunny: \\(x=1,\\;x=2\\);\n  * sunny: \\(y=x-2,\\; y=-2x+9,\\; y=-\\tfrac12x+3.5\\)\n  cover all points of \\(S_5\\), giving \\(k=3\\).\n\nThus the statement \u201cno configuration exists with \\(2\\le k\\le n-1\\)\u201d is invalid.\n\n---\n\n### 13. Construction for \\(k=n\\)\n\n> \u201cFor each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) define the line \\(L_i:\\; y = i\\,x - \\frac{i(i-1)}2 .\\tag{17}\\) \u2026 Substituting \\(i=n+1-a-b\\) \u2026 yields an identity \u2026\u201d\n\n**Issue \u2013 Critical Error.**  \nThe proposed family fails to cover points on the hypotenuse of the triangle. For any point with \\(a+b=n+1\\) we obtain \\(i=0\\), which is not among the defined lines (the index range is \\(1\\le i\\le n\\)). Direct substitution shows many points are not satisfied; for example with \\(n=5\\) the point \\((3,2)\\) gives \\(i=1\\) and the line \\(y=x\\) does **not** pass through \\((3,2)\\). The \u201cstraightforward algebraic verification\u201d claimed in the text is therefore incorrect.\n\n---\n\n### 14. Final classification  \n\n> \u201cHence for every integer \\(n\\ge3\\) the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is \\(\\boxed{k\\in\\{0,\\,1,\\,n\\}}\\).\u201d\n\n**Issue \u2013 Critical Error.**  \nBecause the preceding arguments are flawed, the final answer is not the complete set of admissible \\(k\\). Counter\u201