ght\u2011angled triangle with three sides  \n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   L &: \\{(n-k+1,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le k\\},\\\\\n   B &: \\{(a,1)\\mid n-k+1\\le a\\le n\\},\\\\\n   H &: \\{(a,b)\\mid a+b=n+1,\\; n-k+1\\le a\\le n\\}.\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   Their union contains exactly  \n\n   \\[\n   |L\\cup B\\cup H| = 3k-3\n   \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n   points (the three vertices are counted twice, all other points are distinct).  \n   A sunny line is not parallel to any of the three sides, therefore it can intersect each side in **at most one** point.  Because a line cannot intersect all three non\u2011collinear sides simultaneously, a sunny line meets at most two of the sides, i.e. it contains at most two points of \\(L\\cup B\\cup H\\).  \n   With \\(k\\) sunny lines we can cover at most \\(2k\\) points of the boundary.  Since every boundary point must be covered we need  \n\n   \\[\n   2k\\ge 3k-3\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;k\\le 3 .\n   \\tag{8}\n   \\]\n\n   The inequality (8) is valid for all \\(k\\ge 2\\).  The cases \\(k=0,1\\) are handled separately (they are feasible, see the constructions below).\n\n6. **Impossibility of \\(k=2\\).**  \n   When \\(k=2\\) the bound (8) gives \\(2\\cdot2\\ge 3\\cdot2-3\\), i.e. \\(4\\ge 3\\), which does not rule out \\(k=2\\).  A sharper argument is needed.  \n   After the forced vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-2\\) the uncovered set is  \n\n   \\[\n   U=\\{(n-1,1),\\;(n-1,2),\\;(n,1)\\}.\n   \\tag{9}\n   \\]\n\n   Any line that contains two points of \\(U\\) has slope \\(0\\) (horizontal), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) or \\(-1\\); all three possibilities are non\u2011sunny.  Hence each sunny line can contain **at most one** point of \\(U\\).  With only two sunny lines we can cover at most two points of \\(U\\), leaving one point uncovered \u2013 a contradiction.  Therefore \\(k=2\\) is impossible for every \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\n7. **Existence constructions.**  \n\n   *\\(k=0\\).*  Take the \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\