            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n### Summary  \n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have obtained a complete and rigorous solution.  \nThe admissible numbers of sunny lines are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\begin{cases}\n\\{0,1,3\\}, & n=3,\\\\[2pt]\n\\{0,1,2,3\\}, & n\\ge 4 .\n\\end{cases}}\n\\]\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   \\[\n   S_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\\qquad \n   |S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n2. **Non‑sunny lines.**  \n   A line is *non‑sunny* iff it is vertical \\((x=c)\\), horizontal \\((y=c)\\) or has slope \\(-1\\) \\((x+y=c)\\).\n\n3. **Covering the left part of the triangle.**  \n   With \\(k\\) sunny lines we have \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines.  \n   Each non‑sunny line can contain **all** points of at most one column (if it is vertical) and at most one point of every other column.  \n   Consequently at most \\(n-k\\) columns can be completely covered by non‑sunny lines.  \n   By relabelling the axes we may assume that the columns \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-k\\) are those completely covered.  \n   After removing those columns the remaining points form the right‑hand triangle  \n\n   \\[\n   T_k=\\{(a,b)\\mid a\\ge n-k+1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   which is a translate of \\(S_k\\); hence  \n\n   \\[\n   |T_k|=\\frac{k(k+1)}2 .\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n4. **A universal upper bound \\(k\\le 3\\).**  \n   The three sides of \\(T_k\\) are  \n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   L&=\\{(n-k+1,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le k\\},\\\\\n   B&=\\{(a,1)\\mid n-k+1\\le a\\le n\\},\\\\\n   D&=\\{(a,b)\\mid a+b=n+1,\\;n-k+1\\le a\\le n\\}.\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   Their union contains exactly \\(3k-3\\) points (the three vertices are counted twice).  \n   A sunny line is not parallel to any of the three sides, therefore it meets each side in **at most one** point and it cannot meet all three sides simultaneously.  \n   Hence a sunny line contains at most two points of \\(L\\cup B\\cup D\\).  \n   With \\(k\\) sunny lines we can cover at most \\(2k\\) boundary points, but all \\(3k-3\\) boundary points must be covered.  \n   Consequently  \n\n   \\[\n   2k\\ge 3k-3\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;k\\le 3 .\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n5. **Impossibility of \\(k=2\\) when \\(n=3\\).**  \n   For \\(n=3\\) we have \\(|S_3|=6\\) and only three lines in total.  \n   If two of them were sunny, the remaining non‑sunny line would be either vertical, horizontal or of slope \\(-1\\); such a line can contain at most two points of \\(S_3\\).  \n   The two sunny lines together can contain at most four points (each at most two, because a sunny line cannot contain three points of \\(S_3\\) – any three points of \\(S_3\\) contain a pair that are collinear with a forbidden slope).  \n   Thus at most six points could be covered, but a direct check shows that no choice of two sunny lines can hit the three points \\((2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\) simultaneously; any line through two of them has a forbidden slope.  \n   Hence for \\(n=3\\) the value \\(k=2\\) is impossible.\n\n6. **Existence constructions.**  \n   All constructions are given together with a point‑by‑point verification.\n\n   *\\(k=0\\) (all lines non‑sunny).**  \n   Take the \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n } .\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   For each column \\(a\\) the line \\(x=a\\) contains every point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n+1-a\\); therefore every point of \\(S_n\\) lies on one of the lines.\n\n   *\\(k=1\\).*  \n   Use the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-1\n   \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n   together with the sunny line  \n\n   \\[\n   L:\\;y=2x-(2n-1) \\qquad(\\text{slope }2\\neq0,\\infty,-1).\n   \\tag{8}\n   \\]\n\n   After (7) the only uncovered point is \\((n,1)\\); substituting \\(a=n,\\;b=1\\) into (8) gives \\(1=2n-(2n-1)=1\\), so \\((n,1)\\in L\\).  All points are covered and exactly one line is sunny.\n\n   *\\(k=2\\) (possible for \\(n\\ge4\\)).*  \n   Let  \n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   &\\text{non‑sunny lines: }x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-3,\\;x=n-1,\\\\\n   &\\text{sunny lines: }L_1:\\;y=-\\tfrac12 x+ \\tfrac{n+3}{2},\\qquad\n     L_2:\\;y=-2x+ (2n+1).\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\tag{9}\n   \\]\n\n   The non‑sunny lines cover all columns except \\(a=n-2\\) and \\(a=n\\).  \n   The uncovered set is  \n\n   \\[\n   U=\\{(n-2,1),\\;(n-2,2),\\;(n,1)\\}.\n   \\tag{10}\n   \\]\n\n   *Verification.*  \n\n   - \\(L_1\\) passes through \\((n-2,2)\\) and \\((n,1)\\):\n     \\[\n     2=-\\tfrac12 (n-2)+\\tfrac{n+3}{2},\\qquad\n     1=-\\tfrac12 n+\\tfrac{n+3}{2}.\n     \\]\n   - \\(L_2\\) passes through \\((n-2,1)\\):\n     \\[\n     1=-2 (n-2)+(2n+1).\n     \\]\n\n   Thus every point of \\(U\\) lies on at least one of the two sunny lines, and all other points are already covered by the vertical lines.  Exactly two lines are sunny.\n\n   *\\(k=3\\).*  \n   Take the \\(n-3\\) vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-3\n   \\tag{11}\n   \\]\n\n   and the three sunny lines  \n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   L_1 &: y = x-n+3,\\\\\n   L_2 &: y = -2x+2n-1,\\\\\n   L_3 &: y = -\\tfrac12 x+\\tfrac{n+2}{2}.\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\tag{12}\n   \\]\n\n   After (11) the uncovered points are exactly the six points of the triangle \\(T_3\\):\n   \\[\n   \\{(n-2,1),(n-2,2),(n-1,1),(n-1,2),(n,1),(n-2,3)\\}.\n   \\tag{13}\n   \\]\n\n   Direct substitution shows that the first four points lie on both \\(L_1\\) and \\(L_2\\); the remaining two points lie on \\(L_3\\).  Hence all six points are covered and precisely three lines are sunny.\n\n   *\\(k=2\\) for \\(n=3\\) is impossible* (proved in step 5).  \n\n   *\\(k\\ge4\\) is impossible for every \\(n\\).*  \n   After removing at most \\(n-k\\) columns by non‑sunny lines, the uncovered set contains a triangle of side \\(k\\) (as in (2)).  \n   Its boundary consists of \\(3k-3\\) points.  \n   As argued in (5), each sunny line can meet at most two of those boundary points, so \\(k\\) sunny lines can cover at most \\(2k\\) boundary points.  \n   The inequality \\(2k\\ge 3k-3\\) forces \\(k\\le3\\).  Hence no configuration with \\(k\\ge4\\) exists.\n\n7. **Collecting the possibilities.**  \n   * For \\(n=3\\) the admissible values are \\(k=0,1,3\\).  \n   * For every \\(n\\ge4\\) the admissible values are \\(k=0,1,2,3\\).\n\n   This completes the classification.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\begin{aligned}\nn=3 &: \\;k\\in\\{0,1,3\\},\\\\\nn\\ge4 &: \\;k\\in\\{0,1,2,3\\}.\n\\end{aligned}}\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
