            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n###\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Reduction to a triangle of side \\(k\\)\n\nAssume we have \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of them sunny, covering all points of \\(S_n\\).  \nReplace every non‑sunny line by a vertical line that passes through the same integer points of \\(S_n\\) (if the original line is horizontal or of slope \\(-1\\) it meets each column in at most one point, so a vertical line through a column that is not yet completely covered can be added without losing any covered point).  \nThus we may suppose that the \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines are the vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-k .\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nThese lines cover all points of \\(S_n\\) whose first coordinate is \\(\\le n-k\\).  \nThe uncovered points are exactly\n\n\\[\nT_k=\\{(a,b)\\mid a\\ge n-k+1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nSubtracting the constant vector \\((n-k,0)\\) shows that \\(T_k\\) is a translate of  \n\n\\[\nS_k=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le k+1\\}.\n\\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nConsequently the original problem is equivalent to the following:\n\n> **Reduced problem.**  \n> For a given integer \\(k\\ge0\\) decide whether the set \\(S_k\\) can be covered by exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Small values of \\(k\\)\n\n* **\\(k=0\\).**  The set \\(S_0\\) is empty, so the requirement is vacuous.\n\n* **\\(k=1\\).**  \\(S_1=\\{(1,1)\\}\\).  Any line whose slope is not \\(0,\\infty,-1\\) and which passes through \\((1,1)\\) is sunny; for instance \\(y=2x-1\\).  Hence \\(k=1\\) is possible.\n\n* **\\(k=2\\).**  \\(S_2=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}\\).  \n  Any line containing two of these points is respectively\n  \\[\n  \\begin{aligned}\n  &(1,1),(1,2)  &&\\Rightarrow\\; x=1\\ (\\text{vertical}),\\\\\n  &(1,1),(2,1)  &&\\Rightarrow\\; y=1\\ (\\text{horizontal}),\\\\\n  &(1,2),(2,1)  &&\\Rightarrow\\; \\text{slope }-1 .\n  \\end{aligned}\n  \\]\n  All three possibilities are forbidden, therefore no sunny line can contain two points of \\(S_2\\).  Since we have only two sunny lines, at most four points could be covered, but \\(S_2\\) has three points and each of them would need its own line – impossible.  Hence **\\(k=2\\) is impossible** for every \\(n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  The case \\(k=3\\)\n\nThe set  \n\n\\[\nS_3=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\}\n\\tag{13}\n\\]\n\ncontains six points.  Consider the three sunny lines\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_1 &: y = x,\\\\\nL_2 &: y = -\\tfrac12 x + \\tfrac32,\\\\\nL_3 &: y = -2x + 5 .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{14}\n\\]\n\nA direct substitution shows\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_1 &: (1,1),\\;(2,2),\\\\\nL_2 &: (1,2),\\;(3,1),\\\\\nL_3 &: (1,3),\\;(2,1),\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{15}\n\\]\n\nso the six points of \\(S_3\\) are covered.  All three slopes \\(1,-\\tfrac12,-2\\) differ from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); thus the lines are sunny.\n\nFor a general \\(n\\ge3\\) we translate the picture by the vector \\((n-3,0)\\).  \nDefine\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\ell_1 &: y = x-n+3,\\\\\n\\ell_2 &: y = -\\tfrac12 x + \\tfrac{n+3}{2},\\\\\n\\ell_3 &: y = -2x + 2n-1 .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{16}\n\\]\n\nThe six points of the triangle \\(T_3\\) (the translate of \\(S_3\\)) are\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n&(n-2,1),\\;(n-1,2),\\\\\n&(n-2,2),\\;(n,1),\\\\\n&(n-2,3),\\;(n-1,1),\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{17}\n\\]\n\nand a straightforward check using (16) shows that each of them lies on one of the three lines \\(\\ell_1,\\ell_2,\\ell_3\\).  Hence **\\(k=3\\) is feasible for every \\(n\\ge3\\).**\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Impossibility for \\(k\\ge4\\)\n\nLet \\(k\\ge4\\) and assume that \\(k\\) sunny lines cover all points of \\(S_k\\).  \nThe three sides of the triangle \\(S_k\\) are\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nV &: x=1,\\qquad 1\\le y\\le k,\\\\\nH &: y=1,\\qquad 1\\le x\\le k,\\\\\nD &: x+y=k+1,\\qquad 1\\le x\\le k .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{18}\n\\]\n\nEach side contains exactly \\(k\\) lattice points; the three vertices belong to two sides, so the total number of *boundary* points is\n\n\\[\nB_k = 3k-3 .\n\\tag{19}\n\\]\n\nA sunny line is not parallel to any of the three sides, therefore it meets each side in **at most one** lattice point.  \nIf a sunny line met all three sides, the three intersection points would be collinear.  Solving the linear equations for three such integer points shows that the only possible common slope is \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\), contradicting the definition of a sunny line.  Consequently a sunny line can meet at most **two** sides, i.e. it can contain at most two boundary points.\n\nHence the \\(k\\) sunny lines together can cover at most \\(2k\\) boundary points.  Since every boundary point must be covered we need\n\n\\[\n2k \\ge B_k = 3k-3 \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad k\\le 3 .\n\\tag{20}\n\\]\n\nThus no configuration exists for \\(k\\ge4\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Final constructions for the original problem\n\nRecall that we may use the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-k\\) to eliminate the first \\(n-k\\) columns; the remaining points are a translate of \\(S_k\\).\n\n* **\\(k=0\\).**  Use the \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\).  All points of \\(S_n\\) lie on one of them.\n\n* **\\(k=1\\).**  Use the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\) and the sunny line  \n\n  \\[\n  y = 2x-(2n-1) ,\n  \\tag{21}\n  \\]\n\n  which passes through the only uncovered point \\((n,1)\\).\n\n* **\\(k=3\\).**  Use the \\(n-3\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-3\\) together with the three sunny lines \\(\\ell_1,\\ell_2,\\ell_3\\) defined in (16).  As shown in step 3 they cover exactly the six points of the remaining triangle \\(T_3\\).\n\nAll three families consist of distinct lines, and the numbers of sunny lines are respectively \\(0,1,3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.  Conclusion\n\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge3\\) the only non‑negative integers \\(k\\) for which there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b>0,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\) and having exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,3\\}\\; } .\n\\]\n\n(When \\(n=3\\) the three values are all admissible; for larger \\(n\\) the same three values remain admissible, and no other \\(k\\) is possible.)\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
