e of the three lines \\(\\ell_1,\\ell_2,\\ell_3\\).  Hence **\\(k=3\\) is feasible for every \\(n\\ge3\\).**\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Impossibility for \\(k\\ge4\\)\n\nLet \\(k\\ge4\\) and assume that \\(k\\) sunny lines cover all points of \\(S_k\\).  \nThe three sides of the triangle \\(S_k\\) are\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nV &: x=1,\\qquad 1\\le y\\le k,\\\\\nH &: y=1,\\qquad 1\\le x\\le k,\\\\\nD &: x+y=k+1,\\qquad 1\\le x\\le k .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{18}\n\\]\n\nEach side contains exactly \\(k\\) lattice points; the three vertices belong to two sides, so the total number of *boundary* points is\n\n\\[\nB_k = 3k-3 .\n\\tag{19}\n\\]\n\nA sunny line is not parallel to any of the three sides, therefore it meets each side in **at most one** lattice point.  \nIf a sunny line met all three sides, the three intersection points would be collinear.  Solving the linear equations for three such integer points shows that the only possible common slope is \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\), contradicting the definition of a sunny line.  Consequently a sunny line can meet at most **two** sides, i.e. it can contain at most two boundary points.\n\nHence the \\(k\\) sunny lines together can cover at most \\(2k\\) boundary points.  Since every boundary point must be covered we need\n\n\\[\n2k \\ge B_k = 3k-3 \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad k\\le 3 .\n\\tag{20}\n\\]\n\nThus no configuration exists for \\(k\\ge4\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Final constructions for the original problem\n\nRecall that we may use the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-k\\) to eliminate the first \\(n-k\\) columns; the remaining points are a translate of \\(S_k\\).\n\n* **\\(k=0\\).**  Use the \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\).  All points of \\(S_n\\) lie on one of them.\n\n* **\\(k=1\\).**  Use the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\) and the sunny line  \n\n