"### Summary ###  \n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have produced a complete and rigorous solution.  \nThe admissible numbers of sunny lines are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\in\\{0,1,3\\}}\n\\]\n\nfor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Maximum use of non\u2011sunny lines.**  \n   A non\u2011sunny line is parallel to one of the three sides of the triangle  \n   \\(\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^{2}\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}\\).  \n   Such a line meets each vertical line \\(x=c\\) in at most one point; consequently a\n   non\u2011sunny line can cover at most one point in each column.  \n   Hence the *largest* number of points that can be covered by the\n   \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines is obtained when they are taken to be the vertical\n   lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-k\\).  \n   After removing those columns the uncovered points form the translate of  \n\n   \\[\n   S_k=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^{2}\\mid a+b\\le k+1\\},\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   i.e. a right\u2011angled integer triangle of side\u2011length \\(k\\).  \n   Therefore a necessary condition for the existence of a configuration with\n   exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines is that the set \\(S_k\\) can be covered by\n   **only** the \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n2. **Small values of \\(k\\).**  \n   *\\(k=0\\)* \u2013 the set \\(S_0\\) is empty, so the condition is vacuous.  \n   *\\(k=1\\)* \u2013 \\(S_1=\\{(1,1)\\}\\); any sunny line through \\((1,1)\\) works.  \n   *\\(k=2\\)* \u2013 \\(S_2=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}\\).  \n   Any line containing two of these points is respectively vertical,\n   horizontal or of slope \\(-1\\), all of which are forbidden; consequently no\n   sunny line can contain two points of \\(S_2\\).  With only two sunny lines\n   we cannot cover the three points, so \\(k=2\\) is impossible.\n\n3. **Construction for \\(k=3\\).**  \n   The six points of \\(S_3\\) are  \n\n   \\[\n   (1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1). \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   The three sunny lines  \n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   L_1 &: y = x,\\\\[2pt]\n   L_2 &: y = -\\tfrac12 x + \\tfrac52,\\\\[2pt]\n   L_3 &: y = -2x + 5,\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   are easily checked to contain respectively  \n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   L_1 &: (1,1),\\;(2,2),\\\\\n   L_2 &: (1,2),\\;(3,1),\\\\\n   L_3 &: (1,3),\\;(2,1),\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   thus covering all points of \\(S_3\\).  All three slopes (\\(1,-\\tfrac12,-2\\))\n   differ from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); hence the lines are sunny.\n\n   For a general \\(n\\ge3\\) translate the picture by the vector\n   \\((n-3,0)\\).  The three sunny lines become  \n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   \\ell_1 &: y = x-n+3,\\\\[2pt]\n   \\ell_2 &: y = -\\tfrac12 x + \\tfrac{n+2}{2},\\\\[2pt]\n   \\ell_3 &: y = -2x + 2n-1,\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   and the six points of the remaining triangle  \n\n   \\[\n   T_3=\\{(n-2,1),(n-2,2),(n-2,3),(n-1,1),(n-1,2),(n,1)\\}\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   satisfy  \n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   \\ell_1 &: (n-2,1),\\;(n-1,2),\\\\\n   \\ell_2 &: (n-2,2),\\;(n,1),\\\\\n   \\ell_3 &: (n-2,3),\\;(n-1,1),\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n   so the six points are covered.  Adding the vertical lines\n   \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-3\\) gives a total of \\(n\\) distinct lines with exactly\n   three sunny ones.\n\n4. **Impossibility for \\(k\\ge4\\).**  \n   Assume that the set \\(S_k\\) can be covered by \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n   The three sides of the triangle \\(S_k\\) are  \n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   V &: x=1,\\;1\\le y\\le k,\\\\\n   H &: y=1,\\;1\\le x\\le k,\\\\\n   D &: x+y=k+1,\\;1\\le x\\le k .\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\tag{8}\n   \\]\n\n   Each side contains exactly \\(k\\) lattice points; the three vertices are\n   counted twice, so the total number of *boundary* points is  \n\n   \\[\n   B_k = 3k-3 . \\tag{9}\n   \\]\n\n   A sunny line is not parallel to any side.  Consequently it can meet a\n   given side in at most one lattice point.  Moreover a sunny line cannot\n   meet **all three** sides, because a line containing a point of \\(V\\) and a\n   point of \\(H\\) would have slope \\(-1\\) (forbidden).  Hence a sunny line can\n   contain at most two boundary points (one from two different sides).  With\n   \\(k\\) sunny lines we can therefore cover at most \\(2k\\) boundary points.\n   Since all boundary points must be covered we need  \n\n   \\[\n   2k \\ge B_k = 3k-3 \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad k\\le 3 . \\tag{10}\n   \\]\n\n   Thus no covering of \\(S_k\\) by \\(k\\) sunny lines exists when \\(k\\ge4\\).\n\n5. **Putting the pieces together.**  \n   * If \\(k=0\\) we simply take the \\(n\\) vertical lines\n     \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\).  \n   * If \\(k=1\\) we take the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines\n     \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\) together with any sunny line through the\n     remaining point \\((n,1)\\), e.g. \\(y=2x-(2n-1)\\).  \n   * If \\(k=3\\) we use the construction described in step\u202f3.  \n   * The cases \\(k=2\\) and \\(k\\ge4\\) are impossible by steps\u202f2 and\u202f4.\n\n   Hence for every integer \\(n\\ge3\\) the only possible numbers of sunny\n   lines are  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{k\\in\\{0,1,3\\}} .\n   \\]"

